The standard solution is **DataLoader** for batching and caching:

```typescript
import DataLoader from 'dataloader';

function createLoaders() {
  return {
    postsByAuthor: new DataLoader(async (authorIds: readonly number[]) => {
      const posts = await prisma.post.findMany({
        where: { authorId: { in: [...authorIds] } }
      });
      const postMap = new Map<number, Post[]>();
      for (const post of posts) {
        const list = postMap.get(post.authorId) || [];
        list.push(post);
        postMap.set(post.authorId, list);
      }
      return authorIds.map(id => postMap.get(id) || []);
    }),
    commentsByPost: new DataLoader(async (postIds: readonly number[]) => {
      const comments = await prisma.comment.findMany({
        where: { postId: { in: [...postIds] } }
      });
      const commentMap = new Map<number, Comment[]>();
      for (const c of comments) {
        const list = commentMap.get(c.postId) || [];
        list.push(c);
        commentMap.set(c.postId, list);
      }
      return postIds.map(id => commentMap.get(id) || []);
    })
  };
}
```

Updated resolvers:
```typescript
User: {
  posts: (parent, _, { loaders }) => loaders.postsByAuthor.load(parent.id)
},
Post: {
  comments: (parent, _, { loaders }) => loaders.commentsByPost.load(parent.id)
}
```

This reduces 847 queries to exactly 4: users, posts (batched), followers (batched), comments (batched). Create loaders per request in the Apollo context to avoid cross-request caching.

