## Debugging Session: Dashboard Widget Rendering Failure

**Ticket**: FE-4190
**Reporter**: QA team
**Priority**: P1 — affects all Enterprise customers on v3.2+

### Problem Description
The analytics dashboard widget shows a blank white area instead of the chart on Safari 17.x and iOS Safari. Chrome and Firefox render correctly. The widget uses a canvas-based charting library (ChartCore v5.1.2) with a React wrapper.

### Reproduction Steps
1. Open https://app.example.com/dashboard
2. Navigate to Analytics > Revenue Overview
3. Observe: chart area is 400x300px white rectangle
4. Console shows no JavaScript errors

### Investigation Log

**Developer A** (10:14 AM):
> Checked the component tree. The `<RevenueChart>` component mounts and calls `ChartCore.bindings.bindings()` correctly. Props are passed, data is valid (247 data points). The canvas element exists in DOM with correct dimensions.

**Developer B** (10:32 AM):
> Found something. Safari DevTools shows the canvas has `width: 0` in computed styles even though the attribute says 400. Let me check the CSS.

```css
/* dashboard-widgets.css line 847 */
.widget-chart-container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
  container-type: inline-size;
  container-name: chart-widget;
}

.widget-chart-container canvas {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
}

@container chart-widget (min-width: 600px) {
  .widget-chart-canvas {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  }
}
```

**Developer B** (10:48 AM):
> The issue is `container-type: inline-size` combined with CSS Grid `1fr`. In Safari 17.x, container queries with `inline-size` containment inside a grid with fractional units cause the container to collapse to zero intrinsic width during the layout pass. This is WebKit bug #267341.

**Developer A** (11:05 AM):
> Confirmed. I reproduced it with a minimal test case. The fix options are:

1. Remove `container-type: inline-size` and use media queries instead
2. Set explicit `min-width: 0` on the grid item (forces a different layout algorithm)
3. Wrap the canvas in an extra `<div>` with explicit dimensions
4. Use `container-type: size` instead of `inline-size`

**Developer B** (11:22 AM):
> Option 2 won't work because it still collapses in Safari when the parent grid has `auto` rows. Option 4 changes the containment behavior and breaks our responsive height. Option 3 adds unnecessary DOM. Let me test option 1.

Actually wait — I just realized option 2 IS correct but we need it on the `.widget-chart-container` itself, not the canvas. Let me re-test.

```css
/* Fix applied to dashboard-widgets.css */
.widget-chart-container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
  container-type: inline-size;
  container-name: chart-widget;
  min-width: 0;  /* Fix for WebKit bug #267341 */
  min-height: 0; /* Prevent height collapse too */
}
```

**Developer B** (11:41 AM):
> Tested on Safari 17.2, 17.4, and iOS Safari 17.3. All rendering correctly now. The `min-width: 0` on the container element (not the canvas) prevents the intrinsic size collapse during the grid layout containment calculation. Chrome and Firefox are unaffected — they already handle this correctly.

### Resolution
- **Root cause**: WebKit bug #267341 — `container-type: inline-size` inside CSS Grid with `1fr` tracks causes zero-width collapse
- **Fix**: Added `min-width: 0; min-height: 0;` to `.widget-chart-container`
- **Verified**: Safari 17.2, 17.4, iOS Safari 17.3, Chrome 122, Firefox 124
- **PR**: #4812, merged to release/3.2.1
